WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.100
Terraform workflows depend on more than the helper script itself.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:11.400
They depend on modules, providers, variables, environment overlays, generated configuration, and remote sources.

3
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:21.340
Those inputs can change identity permissions, network exposure, storage settings, logging behavior, encryption choices, and service configuration.

4
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:29.440
A secure helper script should assume that dependency and input governance is part of the workflow, not a separate concern.

5
00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:37.640
Providers and plugins are especially important because they run in automation environments that may have access to credentials and state.

6
00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:44.060
Version changes can affect behavior, supported arguments, defaults, and generated plans.

7
00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:53.200
Organizations should control provider versions through an approved process, pin versions where appropriate, and review updates before they affect privileged workflows.

8
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:57.460
The same principle applies to shared modules and remote module sources.

9
00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:01.580
Variable files and environment overlays deserve careful review.

10
00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:12.480
A small variable change can alter regions, CIDR ranges, public exposure, identity bindings, logging retention, backup behavior, or encryption settings.

11
00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:19.780
Helper scripts should make the selected variable set explicit and should avoid silently combining unrelated overlays.

12
00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:25.880
Reviewers should be able to see which variables were used for a plan and why those variables match the target environment.

13
00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:31.440
Shared modules need ownership, versioning, provenance, and review history.

14
00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:37.700
A module that creates infrastructure for many teams becomes part of the organization's platform boundary.

15
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:45.400
Defaults should be intentional, especially for permissions, network access, storage protection, logging, and monitoring.

16
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:52.140
Secure Terraform helper scripts should reinforce module and provider governance instead of bypassing it for convenience.
